Flexbox simplifies the creation of one‑dimensional layouts that adapt to different screen sizes. By setting a container's display property to flex, its direct children become flex items. The main axis runs horizontally by default, while the cross axis runs vertically. Properties such as justify-content control spacing along the main axis (e.g., space-between, center), and align-items aligns items on the cross axis (e.g., stretch, flex-start). Flex items can grow or shrink using flex-grow and flex-shrink, allowing them to fill available space or shrink when needed. The following example creates a navigation bar that evenly distributes three links across the width of the viewport and collapses them into a vertical stack on narrow screens using a media query:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <style>
    .nav {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-around; /* distribute items */
      background: #2196F3;
      padding: 10px;
    }
    .nav a {
      color: white;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: bold;
    }
    @media (max-width: 500px) {
      .nav {
        flex-direction: column;   /* stack vertically */
        align-items: center;
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <nav class="nav">
    <a href="#home">Home</a>
    <a href="#services">Services</a>
    <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
  </nav>
</body>
</html>
```

Resize the browser window to see the navigation switch from a horizontal row to a vertical column when the width drops below 500px. Flexbox automatically recalculates item sizes, making responsive design more straightforward than using floats or inline‑block techniques.

Node.js uses a module system called CommonJS. Each file is treated as a separate module, and you expose functionality with module.exports while importing with require(). For example, you can create a utility module that exports a function:

```js
// utils.js
function greet(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
module.exports = { greet };
```

In another file you import it:

```js
const { greet } = require('./utils');
console.log(greet('World'));
```

When you need to serve HTTP traffic, the built‑in http module provides a low‑level API. The following code creates a server that responds with "Hello from Node" for every request:

```js
const http = require('http');
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
  res.end('Hello from Node');
});
server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server listening on http://localhost:3000'));
```

Key points: the callback receives request and response objects; writeHead sets status and headers; end sends the body and closes the connection. This pattern forms the basis for more complex frameworks like Express.

Node.js is built around a non‑blocking I/O model. The `fs` module provides both callback‑based and Promise‑based APIs. A callback version reads a file like this:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
fs.readFile('data.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Read error:', err);
    return;
  }
  console.log('File contents:', data);
});
```

If you prefer Promises, you can use `fs.promises`:

```js
const { readFile } = require('fs').promises;
async function showFile() {
  try {
    const data = await readFile('data.txt', 'utf8');
    console.log('File contents:', data);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Read error:', e);
  }
}
showFile();
```

Both approaches avoid blocking the event loop. The callback style can lead to "callback hell" when many steps are chained, while async/await with Promises yields clearer, linear‑looking code. Understanding both is essential because many legacy libraries still expose callback APIs.

React encourages building UI as a tree of reusable components. A functional component is a plain JavaScript function that returns JSX, a syntax extension that looks like HTML but is compiled to `React.createElement` calls. Example of a greeting component:

```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
}

// Usage in another component
function App() {
  return <Greeting name="Alice" />;
}
```

JSX must have a single root element, so you often wrap multiple elements in a `<div>` or React fragment (`<>...</>`). Props are read‑only inputs; they flow from parent to child. When a prop changes, React re‑renders the affected component tree efficiently using its virtual DOM diffing algorithm.

React's Hooks let functional components hold mutable state and run side effects. `useState` returns a state variable and a setter function. Example of a counter:

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Current: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`useEffect` runs after render and can clean up resources. A common pattern is fetching data on mount:

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
function DataFetcher() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/info')
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(setData)
      .catch(console.error);
  }, []); // empty dependency array => run once
  return data ? <pre>{JSON.stringify(data, null, 2)}</pre> : <p>Loading...</p>;
}
```

The dependency array tells React when to re‑run the effect; omitting it runs the effect after every render, which can cause infinite loops if the effect updates state.

Arrays store elements in contiguous memory, allowing O(1) random access by index. However, inserting or deleting in the middle requires shifting elements, leading to O(n) time. Linked lists consist of nodes where each node holds a value and a reference to the next node. This structure enables O(1) insertion or deletion at the head, but random access requires traversing nodes sequentially, resulting in O(n) time. Below is a simple JavaScript implementation of a singly linked list with methods to append a node and to print the list:

```javascript
class ListNode {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
  }
}

class SinglyLinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
  }

  append(value) {
    const newNode = new ListNode(value);
    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = newNode;
      return;
    }
    let curr = this.head;
    while (curr.next) {
      curr = curr.next;
    }
    curr.next = newNode;
  }

  print() {
    let curr = this.head;
    const values = [];
    while (curr) {
      values.push(curr.value);
      curr = curr.next;
    }
    console.log(values.join(' -> '));
  }
}
```
Understanding these trade‑offs is essential for selecting the appropriate structure for a given problem.

Binary search repeatedly halves a sorted array to locate a target value. At each step, it compares the target with the middle element; if they differ, the search continues in the appropriate half. This yields a logarithmic O(log n) time complexity. Recursion naturally expresses this process: a function calls itself with a reduced sub‑array until a base case is reached. Below is a recursive JavaScript implementation of binary search that returns the index of the target or -1 if not found:

```javascript
function binarySearch(arr, target, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left > right) return -1; // base case: not found
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  if (arr[mid] === target) return mid; // found
  if (arr[mid] > target) {
    return binarySearch(arr, target, left, mid - 1);
  } else {
    return binarySearch(arr, target, mid + 1, right);
  }
}
```
Recursion must have a clear base case to avoid infinite loops, and each recursive call reduces the problem size, guaranteeing termination. Mastering this pattern prepares you for many classic algorithms such as quicksort and depth‑first search.

RESTful services rely on the semantics of HTTP. GET retrieves resources without side effects and should be safe and idempotent, returning 200 (OK) on success. POST creates a new resource, often returning 201 (Created) along with a Location header pointing to the new URI. PUT updates an existing resource and is idempotent; repeated identical requests yield the same state, typically returning 200 (OK) or 204 (No Content). DELETE removes a resource and is also idempotent, returning 200 (OK), 202 (Accepted), or 204 (No Content) when successful. Below is a minimal Express.js server demonstrating each method with appropriate status codes:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

let items = [];

app.get('/items', (req, res) => {
  res.status(200).json(items);
});

app.post('/items', (req, res) => {
  const newItem = req.body;
  items.push(newItem);
  res.status(201).location(`/items/${items.length - 1}`).json(newItem);
});

app.put('/items/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = Number(req.params.id);
  if (id < 0 || id >= items.length) return res.sendStatus(404);
  items[id] = req.body;
  res.sendStatus(204);
});

app.delete('/items/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = Number(req.params.id);
  if (id < 0 || id >= items.length) return res.sendStatus(404);
  items.splice(id, 1);
  res.sendStatus(204);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('API listening on port 3000'));
```
Understanding these conventions ensures clients can correctly interpret responses and interact predictably with the API.

A clean REST API uses nouns for resources and hierarchical paths to express relationships. For example, `/users` represents the collection of user entities, while `/users/42` addresses the specific user with ID 42. Responses should be JSON objects or arrays, and error conditions should include meaningful status codes and messages. Below is an Express.js example that defines a GET endpoint returning a user object if found, or a 404 error otherwise:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }
];

app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const userId = Number(req.params.id);
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === userId);
  if (!user) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
  }
  res.status(200).json(user);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running'));
```
Notice the use of `res.status(...).json(...)` to set both the HTTP status and the response body. Consistent URL design and response formatting make APIs easier to consume and document.

MongoDB stores data as BSON documents, which are JSON‑like structures that can contain nested objects and arrays. Each document resides in a collection, and collections are analogous to tables in relational databases but without a fixed schema. The `_id` field is automatically added to every document and serves as a unique identifier; if omitted, MongoDB generates an ObjectId. CRUD operations are performed via methods such as `insertOne`, `find`, `updateOne`, and `deleteOne`. Because the schema is flexible, you can add new fields to documents without affecting existing ones, enabling rapid iteration. However, careful design of indexes—especially on fields frequently queried—remains essential for performance. The driver returns Promises, allowing async/await syntax for clean code.

Example:
```js
const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');
const client = new MongoClient('mongodb://localhost:27017');
await client.connect();
const db = client.db('shop');
const products = db.collection('products');
await products.insertOne({ name: 'Laptop', price: 1299.99, inStock: true });
const item = await products.findOne({ name: 'Laptop' });
console.log(item);
await client.close();
```

The aggregation framework processes data through a sequence of stages, each transforming the documents. Common stages include `$match` for filtering, `$group` for grouping and calculating aggregates, `$project` for reshaping, and `$sort` for ordering. Indexes can accelerate `$match` and `$sort` stages, especially when the pipeline begins with a `$match` that can be satisfied by an index. For example, an index on `category` speeds up a `$match` that filters by that field. The `$group` stage can use the `_id` field to define grouping keys, and accumulator operators like `$sum`, `$avg`, and `$push` compute results. Understanding the order of stages and index usage is key to building performant pipelines.

Example:
```js
const sales = db.collection('sales');
await sales.createIndex({ productId: 1, saleDate: 1 });
const pipeline = [
  { $match: { saleDate: { $gte: new Date('2023-01-01') } } },
  { $group: { _id: '$productId', totalQty: { $sum: '$quantity' } } },
  { $sort: { totalQty: -1 } }
];
const results = await sales.aggregate(pipeline).toArray();
console.log(results);
```
